                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Flawed cinematic detective thriller held back by frustrating action gimmicks.\r\n\r\nIf you are thinking of picking up Sherlock Holmes: The Devil's Daughter, it is a visually impressive entry in Frogwares' lineup, but still far from perfect. While the deeply emotional narrative surrounding Holmes' daughter and the classic deduction boards will keep you hooked, the game forces several jarring design shifts that might test your patience.\r\n\r\nHere is what you need to know before diving in:\r\n\r\n• Obnoxious Quick-Time Events: The game replaces pure detective work with forced action sequences. From balancing on logs to frantic chase sequences and stealth segments, these clunky mechanics feel entirely out of place in a Sherlock Holmes game and disrupt the pacing.\r\n\r\n• Superfluous Open World: While Victorian London looks beautiful and atmospheric, the open-world streets are completely empty and devoid of meaningful interactions. It serves as nothing more than a tedious, slow-paced transition corridor between actual crime scenes.\r\n\r\n• Satisfying Deduction Board: The core investigation mechanics remain excellent. Piecing together clues, linking thoughts in Sherlock's mind palace, and having the freedom to choose your own culprit—and decide their moral fate—is easily the strongest part of the game.\r\n\r\n• Shifting Narrative Tone: The overarching story about Sherlock's adoptive daughter, Kate, provides a fascinating, high-stakes personal angle, though the final act takes a dramatic, supernatural-leaning turn that splits the community on whether it lands the ending.\r\n\r\nFinal Verdict: A solid, narrative-driven detective experience if you can overlook the mandatory, frustrating mini-games. It doesn't quite capture the pure mystery perfection of Crimes & Punishments, but it is well worth a look—just put it on your wishlist and grab it on a deep discount.",
